--TEST--
an `autoload` prefix holding a segment that is not a namespace segment does not compile — `rule:programs/autoload`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:programs/autoload-find-a-class-by-its-namespace
// A prefix is matched segment by segment against written names, and a name's
// segments are `PascalCase` identifiers. `App\*` is what a PSR-4 habit writes
// first, and `App\modules` is a lower-case segment: neither can match a name,
// so each is reported at its declaration instead of as an `E0303` on the first
// class it was meant to find.
//
// A `{..}` segment is held to the same shape once it is read off the disk:
// `{.}` in `modules/boot.nvs` names `modules`, which is lower-case. And one
// prefix takes one directory segment at most. All four come from one run.
require './bootstrap.nvs';
require './modules/boot.nvs';
--FILE bootstrap.nvs--
<?nvs
autoload 'App\*' from './*/src';
autoload 'App\modules' from './modules';
--FILE modules/boot.nvs--
<?nvs
autoload 'App\{.}' from './';
autoload 'App\{..}\{.}' from './';
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0327]: `App\*` is not a namespace prefix
%A
error[E0327]: `App\modules` is not a namespace prefix
%A
error[E0327]: `App\{.}` is not a namespace prefix
%A
%s`{.}` names the directory `modules`, which is not a namespace segment
%A
error[E0327]: `App\{..}\{.}` is not a namespace prefix
%A
%sonly one segment may name a directory
%A
error: aborting due to 4 errors
To read what a code means and how to fix it, run `nvs agent show <code>`.
